  "Question": "The slope of the line \\(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{y}{2}=1\\) is",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-\\frac32\\)",
    "B": "\\(-\\frac23\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\frac13\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\frac23\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\frac32\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Clear the denominators to make the coefficients easier to read. Multiplying the equation by \\(6\\) gives\n\\[\n2x+3y-6=0.\n\\]\nFor a line \\(Ax+By+C=0\\) with \\(B\\ne0\\), solving for \\(y\\) gives\n\\[\ny=-\\frac ABx-\\frac CB.\n\\]\nThe coefficient of \\(x\\) is its slope. Here \\(A=2\\) and \\(B=3\\ne0\\), so the slope is \\(\\boxed{-\\frac23}\\), choice \\(\\text{B}\\).",
